^languages
English (King James Version), Spanish (Reina-Valera 1909), Mandarin Chinese (Chinese Union Version, simplified characters), Hebrew (Westminster Leningrad Codex)
^languages/
^source
Genesis, chapters 1 to 3, one passage per verse: 80 verses in each of four languages. Chosen because it is among the most widely translated and most widely known texts there is, because these editions are in the public domain, and because the verse numbering aligns every language to every other.
English: King James Version, public domain outside the United Kingdom. Spanish: Reina-Valera 1909, public domain. Mandarin: Chinese Union Version (1919), public domain. All three taken from the open-bibles collection (github.com/seven1m/open-bibles).
Hebrew: Westminster Leningrad Codex, public domain, as published by the Open Scriptures Hebrew Bible project (github.com/openscriptures/morphhb), with vowel points and cantillation marks.
Every version is complete and unabridged. The Hebrew is the original; the other three translate it. Markup and editorial footnotes were removed; no word of any verse was changed. In the English, square brackets mark words the King James translators added that have no counterpart in the Hebrew; the 1611 printing set them in different type. The Hebrew keeps its paragraph markers (the letters pe and samekh standing alone at the end of a verse). The chapter headings were supplied for this example.
^source/

